In this section we examine some examples, and attempt to convince the reader
that the information contained in the module $H_0 (cal(F)_M)$ is just the
information recoverable from *local information*#idx("information, local")#idx(
  "local",
  "information",
) about the structure of $M$. This idea will be made more formal in the ensuing
sections. It should also become apparent that finding $H_0 (cal(F))$ for a
coefficient system $cal(F)$ is equivalent to the *embedding problem*#idx(
  "embedding problem",
): given an abstract incidence structure of points, lines, planes etc. defined
by the subgroup structure of the group, when is there a projective space in
which they embed? From this point of view, the statement that the natural module
$M$ for $GL_n (k)$ is equal to $H_0 (cal(F)_M)$ says that we can #source(
  256,
)recover projective space from the incidence structure of its points and lines.
This can be thought of as a weak form of the fundamental theorem of projective
geometry. Our first example shows that the corresponding theorem for symplectic
geometry is only true in odd characteristic; in characteristic two, we end up
constructing an orthogonal space of one larger dimension.

#subhead[The symplectic and odd dimensional orthogonal groups] We now examine
the $2n$-dimensional natural module $M$ for the symplectic group
$Sp_(2n) (FF_q)$#idx("$Sp_(2n) (FF_q)$") ($n >= 2$). As mentioned in the section
on Chevalley groups (Section~@sec:chevalley-groups), this is the group
associated to the $C_n$ Dynkin diagram over the field $FF_q$.

Recall that an *isotropic subspace*#idx("isotropic", "subspace")#idx(
  "subspace, isotropic",
) of a symplectic space is a subspace on which the symplectic form vanishes
identically. The parabolic subgroups of the symplectic group are just the
stabilisers of flags of isotropic subspaces (*isotropic flags*#idx(
  "isotropic",
  "flag",
)#idx("flag", "isotropic")). Thus the Borel subgroups are the stabilisers of
complete isotropic flags $0 < V_1 < dots.c < V_n$ with $dim(V_i) = i$. The
maximal parabolics (which are as usual labelled by the vertices of the Dynkin
diagram) correspond to isotropic subspaces according to the following diagram.
$ #isotropic-dynkin $

Thus the building of $G$ has as vertices the isotropic subspaces, as edges the
isotropic flags of length 2, etc. up to $(n-1)$-simplices corresponding to the
complete isotropic flags. In other words, this is just the simplicial
realisation of the poset of non-zero isotropic subspaces of the symplectic space
$M$.

The coefficient system $cal(F)_M$ assigns to each simplex the smallest subspace
in the corresponding flag, since this is the fixed space of the $O_p$ of the
corresponding parabolic subgroup. Thus this coefficient system is the one
obtained by the usual process from the coefficient system on the poset of
non-zero isotropic subspaces which assigns to each element of the poset the
corresponding isotropic subspace. We call this the *tautological coefficient
system*#idx("tautological coefficient system")#idx(
  "coefficient system",
  "tautological",
). So $cal(F)_M$ is the simplicial realisation of the tautological coefficient
system.

What is $H_0 (cal(F)_M)$? Since two distinct isotropic 1-spaces are contained in
an isotropic subspace if and only if they are contained in an isotropic 2-space,
the general construction of limits shows that $H_0 (cal(F)_M)$ is the quotient
of the direct sum of all the isotropic 1-spaces by the relators coming from the
isotropic 2-spaces. If $v$ is an isotropic vector in $M$, we shall write
$hat(v)$ for the corresponding element of the corresponding 1-space in
$H_0 (cal(F)_M)$. In these terms, $H_0 (cal(F)_M)$ is generated by the $hat(v)$
for isotropic points $v in M$ subject to the relations
$hat(v) = hat(w) + hat(x)$ whenever $v = w + x$ and $v$, $w$ and $x$ lie in an
isotropic 2-space. The surjection $H_0 (cal(F)_M) -> M$ takes $hat(v)$ to $v$.

#theorem[
  + If $k$ has odd characteristic then $H_0 (cal(F)_M) tilde.equiv M$.
  + #source(257)If $k$ has characteristic two then there is a short exact
    sequence
    $ 0 -> k -> H_0 (cal(F)_M) -> M -> 0. $
] <th:symplectic-fixed-point-homology>

#proof(closed: false)[
  + Choose isotropic subspaces $X = chevron.l x_1, dots, x_n chevron.r$ and
    $Y = chevron.l y_1, dots, y_n chevron.r$ of dimension $n$ with
    $(x_i, y_j) = delta_(i j)$. Then $hat(X)$ and $hat(Y)$ are $n$-dimensional
    isotropic subspaces of $H_0 (cal(F)_M)$, and we must show that each
    $hat(z) in H_0 (cal(F)_M)$ is in the linear span of $hat(X)$ and $hat(Y)$.
    Let $z = x + y$ with $x in X$ and $y in Y$. If $(x, y) = 0$, we are done, so
    assume $(x, y) = a != 0$. Choose $x' in X$ with $(x', y) = 0$. Since $k$ has
    odd characteristic, we may define
    $
       x_0 & = inline(1/2) (x + x') &  quad x & = x_0 + x'_0 \
      x'_0 & = inline(1/2) (x - x') & quad x' & = x_0 - x'_0
    $
    Choose $y_0$ with $(x_0, y_0) = 0$ and $(x'_0, y_0) = a slash 2$, and let
    $y'_0 = y - y_0$. Then
    $
      (x_0 + y_0, x'_0 + y'_0) = (x_0, y'_0) + (y_0, x'_0)
      = a slash 2 - a slash 2 = 0
    $
    and
    $ z = (x_0 + y_0) + (x'_0 + y'_0) $
    so that
    $
      chevron.l hat(z) chevron.r subset.eq
      chevron.l hat((x_0 + y_0)), hat((x'_0 + y'_0)) chevron.r subset.eq
      chevron.l hat(x)_0, hat(y)_0, hat(x)'_0, hat(y)'_0 chevron.r.
    $
  + In order to understand the situation in characteristic two, it is necessary
    to say something about orthogonal forms in characteristic two. An orthogonal
    form in characteristic two consists of the following data. First of all we
    are given a symmetric bilinear form#idx("bilinear form, symmetric")#idx(
      "form",
      "symmetric bilinear",
    )#idx("symmetric", "bilinear form") $b(x, y) = b(y, x)$. Then we are also
    given a *quadratic form*#idx("quadratic form")#idx("form", "quadratic")
    $q(x)$ which is a map satisfying
    $ q(x + y) = q(x) + q(y) + b(x, y). $
    In particular this implies that $b(x, x) = 0$, so that $b(x, y)$ is a
    symplectic form. In odd dimension, this symplectic form necessarily has a
    non-zero radical, and this radical is invariant under the action of the
    orthogonal group. If the orthogonal form is non-degenerate, the radical can
    only have one dimension, since the quadratic form is (semi)linear on this
    radical. The quotient of the $(2n + 1)$-dimensional orthogonal space by the
    radical of the symplectic form is thus a non-degenerate $(2n)$-dimensional
    symplectic space. Given a (necessarily isotropic) vector in the symplectic
    space, among its preimages is exactly one isotropic vector in the orthogonal
    space. Thus there is a surjective map from $H_0 (cal(F)_M)$ onto an
    orthogonal space of one larger dimension. Case (ii) of the theorem will
    therefore follow from the next theorem, which is independent of
    characteristic.
]

#theorem[
  Let $V$ be a non-degenerate orthogonal $(2n + 1)$-dimensional space
  ($n >= 2$), and let $cal(F)_V$ be the tautological coefficient system on the
  poset of non-trivial isotropic subspaces of $V$. Then the natural map
  $H_0 (cal(F)_V) -> V$ is an isomorphism.
] <th:orthogonal-tautological-homology>

#proof[
  #source(258)Choose a basis $x_1, dots, x_n, y_1, dots, y_n, t$ with
  $X = chevron.l x_1, dots, x_n chevron.r$ and
  $Y = chevron.l y_1, dots, y_n chevron.r$ isotropic, $(x_i, y_j) = delta_(i j)$
  and $q(t) = 0$.
  $ mat(augment: #(hline: 2, vline: 2), 0, I, ?; I, 0, ?; ?, ?, 0) $
  (#smallcaps[Exercise]: Show this is always possible; you'll need to use the
  finiteness of the field.)

  We must show that
  $H_0 (cal(F)_V) = hat(X) plus.o hat(Y) plus.o chevron.l hat(t) chevron.r$;
  i.e., if $x + y + lambda t$ is singular then $hat((x + y + lambda t))
  in hat(X) plus.o hat(Y) plus.o chevron.l hat(t) chevron.r$.

  #smallcaps[Case 1.] If $lambda = 0$ then $x + y$ is singular and so
  $hat((x + y)) = hat(x) + hat(y)$.

  #smallcaps[Case 2.] If $lambda != 0$ then choose a singular 2-space
  $S supset.eq chevron.l x + y + lambda t chevron.r$. By dimension counting,
  $S inter (X plus.o Y)$ is one dimensional, so let it be generated by the
  isotropic vector $s$. By case 1, $hat(s) in hat(X) plus.o hat(Y)$. By a
  similar dimension count, $t^perp$ is a $(2n)$-dimensional subspace
  intersecting $S$ in a one dimensional isotropic subspace. This 1-space,
  together with $t$, generate an isotropic 2-space which intersects $X plus.o Y$
  in an isotropic 1-space. Thus $t^perp inter S
  subset.eq hat(X) plus.o hat(Y) plus.o chevron.l hat(t) chevron.r$, and hence
  also $chevron.l x + y + lambda t chevron.r
  subset.eq hat(X) plus.o hat(Y) plus.o chevron.l hat(t) chevron.r$.
]

#corollary[
  $Sp_(2n) (FF_q) tilde.equiv O_(2n+1) (FF_q)$#idx("$Sp_(2n) (FF_q)$")#idx(
    "$O_(2n+1) (FF_q)$",
  )#idx("group", "symplectic", "$Sp_(2n) (FF_q)$")#idx(
    "symplectic group",
    "$Sp_(2n) (FF_q)$",
  )#idx("group", "orthogonal", "$O_(2n+1) (FF_q)$")#idx(
    "orthogonal group",
    "$O_(2n+1) (FF_q)$",
  ) for $q$ a power of $2$.
] <cor:symplectic-orthogonal-isomorphism>

#proof[
  The above theorem shows that there is an action of $Sp_(2n) (FF_q)$ on the
  $(2n + 1)$-dimensional orthogonal space $H_0 (cal(F)_M)$, so that the natural
  map $O_(2n+1) (FF_q) -> Sp_(2n) (FF_q)$ is surjective. On the other hand, if
  an orthogonal matrix acts trivially on the symplectic quotient space, then it
  fixes the unique isotropic lift of each vector in this symplectic space. But
  these lifts generate the orthogonal space.
]

#remark(kind: "Comment")[
  Ronan and Smith [@bib:Ronan1985] have proved more generally that for any
  *minimum weight*#idx("minimum weight module")#idx("module", "minimum weight")
  module (i.e., one in which there is only one orbit of weights under the action
  of the Weyl group) except for the natural module for $Sp_(2n) (FF_q)$ in
  characteristic two, one has $H_0 (cal(F)_M) = M$. This condition includes the
  natural modules for the symplectic and orthogonal groups, as well as the spin
  modules for the orthogonal groups, the exterior powers of the natural module
  for the linear groups, the 27-dimensional module for type $E_6$ and the
  56-dimensional module for type $E_7$.
]
